ant), technical competence. Concerns: memory for numbers (could affect calculations), nasty remarks (crew cooperation). So mixed.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. Strengths: situational awareness, big picture, leadership. Concerns: attitude (show-off, not open). Could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Concerns: hot temper, know-it-all attitude (crew cooperation). So many candidates have cooperation concerns.\n\nWe need to start discussion. Perhaps pick Candidate B as initial recommendation because strong reliability and stress handling, but note concerns about interpersonal behavior. Or pick Candidate C for anticipation and leadership but attitude concerns. Which is stronger overall? Let's weigh: reliability and stress are critical. B is conscientious, handles stress, good weather assessment, but nasty remarks could be serious for crew. D has hot temper, also problematic. A has egocentric and communication issues. C has show-off and not open to new ideas. All have some concerns. Which candidate has the least severe concerns? B's nasty remarks could be severe. D's hot temper also severe. C's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability. A's egocentric and communication difficulty also severe. So maybe B is still best because strengths align with reliability and stress, and memory for numbers maybe mitigated with tools. So I will propose Candidate B as current recommendation, but note need to discuss concerns.\n\nThus public message: state recommendation for B, reference that no discussion yet, bring up strengths and concerns, suggest we need to compare with others.\n\nProceed."}
